; AX3054: an effect declared with a built-in effect's name, and the
; retirement of the `Err` phantom.
;
; `(effect IO (emit :: (-> Int Int)))` was ACCEPTED, and the acceptance
; was useless in a way nothing reported. A handle list resolves a
; built-in name to the BUILT-IN, so the custom effect can never be
; handled. Measured before this code existed, on that declaration with
; `(handle (emit 1) (IO) h)` around its own call:
;
;   E AX3011 effect mismatch: unhandled effect `IO`
;
; at the handle - the list named the built-in, the body performs the
; custom one - and, since AX3053, a second report at `main` saying the
; operation reaches no handler. Two diagnostics, both pointing away
; from the name that caused them. An untagged `main` performing it
; reads `#effects=IO` with no `#effect=io` and draws nothing at all, so
; the row says the program reaches the outside world when it does
; nothing of the kind.
;
; The reserved set is `isBuiltinEffect`'s, which is the same question a
; handle list asks, so the two cannot drift: `IO`, `Pure`, `Alloc`,
; `Mut`, `Div`, and the lowercase `alloc` the checker also answers for.
; `Err` is NOT in it any more.
;
;   IO       an effect declared with a built-in's name  -> AX3054
;   alloc    the lowercase spelling, same answer        -> AX3054
;   Err      a handle list naming the retired phantom   -> AX3016
;   Console  an ordinary effect, handled                -> silent
;
; THE `Err` PHANTOM. `Err` was accepted as a sixth built-in effect name
; - a handle list could write it - and nothing in the compiler ever
; inferred it. Measured before its removal: `(handle 5 (Err) 0)`
; checked `OK`, and `;@axiom:effect(err)` drew `AX3010 missing err`,
; because the AXTAG path lowercases the value and finds no declaration,
; so the one spelling that resolved to the name and the one that did
; not disagreed about whether it existed. A name a list may write, that
; nothing produces, and that its own tag spelling cannot reach, is not
; a built-in effect; it is a hole in a table. `AX3016` is what a handle
; list naming something undeclared has always drawn.
(effect IO
  (emit :: (-> Int Int)))

(effect alloc
  (grab :: (-> Int Int)))

(effect Console
  (log :: (-> String Int)))

(:: viaConsole (-> Int Int))

(fn (viaConsole n)
  (cast Int (handle (log "covered") (Console) (lambda (s) 0)))
)

(:: viaErr (-> Int Int))

(fn (viaErr n) (cast Int (handle n (Err) 0)))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (+ (viaConsole 1) (viaErr 2)))
